## Proof

Place $M$ at the origin and $AD$ on the $x$-axis with $A=(-1,0)$, $D=(1,0)$, so $MA=MD=1$. Let $\angle DAB=\angle ADC=\alpha$, and set $\gamma=\cos\alpha$, $\sigma=\sin\alpha$.

Since ray $AB$ makes angle $\alpha$ with $\overrightarrow{AD}$ and ray $DC$ makes angle $\alpha$ with $\overrightarrow{DA}$, there exist $p,q>0$ with
$$B=(-1+p\gamma,\;p\sigma),\qquad C=(1-q\gamma,\;q\sigma).$$
Then the midpoints are
$$L=\bigl(-1+\tfrac{p\gamma}{2},\;\tfrac{p\sigma}{2}\bigr),\quad N=\bigl(1-\tfrac{q\gamma}{2},\;\tfrac{q\sigma}{2}\bigr),\quad M=(0,0).$$

**Finding $E=AC\cap BD$.** Parametrize $AC$ by $A+u(C-A)$ and $BD$ by $B+v(D-B)$. Solving the two line equations gives
$$u=\frac{p}{D_0},\qquad D_0:=p+q-pq\gamma,$$
and hence
$$E=\Bigl(\frac{p-q}{D_0},\;\frac{pq\,\sigma}{D_0}\Bigr).$$

**Finding $F$.** The condition $ME\cdot MF=MA^2=1$ means $F$ is the inverse of $E$ in the unit circle centered at $M$. Using complex coordinates with $z_M=0$ and $w:=(p-q)+i\,pq\sigma$, we have $z_E=w/D_0$, so
$$z_F=\frac{z_E}{|z_E|^2}=\frac{D_0\,w}{|w|^2}=\frac{D_0}{\overline w}.$$

**Bisector criterion.** The equality $\angle LFM=\angle MFN$ (i.e. $FM$ bisects $\angle LFN$) is equivalent to
$$R:=\frac{(z_M-z_F)^2}{(z_L-z_F)(z_N-z_F)}\in\mathbb R_{>0}.$$
Indeed, writing $\theta_X=\arg(z_X-z_F)$, this ratio being positive real means $2\theta_M\equiv\theta_L+\theta_N\pmod{2\pi}$, i.e. $\theta_M$ is the midpoint of $\theta_L,\theta_N$ — exactly the (internal) bisector condition.

Since $z_M=0$ and $z_F=D_0/\bar w$,
$$z_L-z_F=\frac{z_L\bar w-D_0}{\bar w}=-\frac{D_0-z_L\bar w}{\bar w},\qquad z_N-z_F=-\frac{D_0-z_N\bar w}{\bar w},$$
so
$$R=\frac{D_0^2/\bar w^2}{(D_0-z_L\bar w)(D_0-z_N\bar w)/\bar w^2}=\frac{D_0^2}{(D_0-z_L\bar w)(D_0-z_N\bar w)}.$$

**Key computation.** With $\bar w=(p-q)-i\,pq\sigma$ and $z_L=(-1+\tfrac{p\gamma}{2})+\tfrac{ip\sigma}{2}$:
$$\operatorname{Im}(z_L\bar w)=(-1+\tfrac{p\gamma}{2})(-pq\sigma)+\tfrac{p\sigma}{2}(p-q)=\frac{\sigma p}{2}\,D_0,$$
$$D_0-\operatorname{Re}(z_L\bar w)=\frac{p}{2}\bigl[4-\gamma(p+q)-pq\sigma^2\bigr].$$
Setting
$$U:=4-\gamma(p+q)-pq\sigma^2,\qquad V:=\sigma D_0\qquad(\text{both real}),$$
we obtain
$$D_0-z_L\bar w=\frac{p}{2}(U-iV).$$

The analogous computation with $z_N=(1-\tfrac{q\gamma}{2})+\tfrac{iq\sigma}{2}$ gives
$$\operatorname{Im}(z_N\bar w)=-\frac{\sigma q}{2}\,D_0,\qquad D_0-\operatorname{Re}(z_N\bar w)=\frac{q}{2}\,U,$$
hence
$$D_0-z_N\bar w=\frac{q}{2}(U+iV).$$

**Conclusion.** Multiplying,
$$(D_0-z_L\bar w)(D_0-z_N\bar w)=\frac{pq}{4}(U-iV)(U+iV)=\frac{pq}{4}(U^2+V^2)\in\mathbb R_{>0},$$
since $p,q>0$ and $U^2+V^2>0$ (non-degeneracy). Therefore
$$R=\frac{D_0^2}{\tfrac{pq}{4}(U^2+V^2)}\in\mathbb R_{>0}.$$

By the bisector criterion, $\angle LFM=\angle MFN$. $\blacksquare$

